    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-28492",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=\\frac{1}{5}(x+1)(x+2)(x-5)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a positive cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}(x+1)(x+2)(x-5)=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x+⋯=\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is already in factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are $x=-1,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=5.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{5}(0+1)(0+2)(0-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)(2)(-5)}{5} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-2,-1,5$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-2,$ as follows:\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234519/assets/e27e18b8e79c71dcc888e62c4472723b44b1b4fa0367e124313f95bc08ef70fa.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
